@meta
id: 07-14
title: Paging over what you do not need to read
chapter: 07
type: lesson
skills: scroll-half
par: 55
minutes: 6
vscode: drag the scrollbar and overshoot
open: seed.sql

@brief
A dumped seed file, 173 lines, three `INSERT` statements with fifty-odd
value rows each. All three statements are missing their terminating
semicolon, which is on the last value row of each block.

Put a `;` on the end of the last row of each of the three statements.

The rows themselves are generated and there is nothing in them to read.
This is the other kind of travel: not reading your way down, but getting
past a slab of text you have no interest in.

@keys
| `Ctrl-F` | forward one whole screen |
| `Ctrl-B` | back one whole screen |
| `Ctrl-D` `Ctrl-U` | half a screen, when you do need to read |
| `G` | last line |
| `k` `A` | up a line, append |

@hints
`Ctrl-D` was half a screen because you were reading. Here you are not
reading, so take whole screens.
--
`Ctrl-F` is forward a full screen, `Ctrl-B` is back a full screen. Page
until the blank line between two statements appears, then step onto the row
above it.
--
The third statement ends on the last line of the file, and you already have
a key for that. Do not page to somewhere you can name.

@solution
How many pages it takes depends on how tall your window is. On a short one:

    Ctrl-F Ctrl-F Ctrl-F   6k   A ; <Esc>
    Ctrl-F Ctrl-F Ctrl-F    j   A ; <Esc>
    G A ; <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Twenty-three keystrokes, most of them one finger on Ctrl-F. If a page takes
you past the gap, `Ctrl-B` brings the whole screen back and costs you one
key.

`Ctrl-F` and `Ctrl-B` are Page Down and Page Up, and they are the one place
in vim where the keys you brought with you from every other editor are
genuinely the same idea. The reason to use the Ctrl versions anyway is that
your hands stay on the home row, which matters more than it sounds when the
next thing you do is `k` and `A`.

Choosing between the half screen and the full screen is a real choice and it
is about your eyes, not about speed. A full screen leaves you nothing you
have already seen, so use it when you are skipping. A half screen overlaps,
so use it when you are looking for something. Skipping past generated rows
is the full-screen case.

The third fix is the real lesson though. Paging to the bottom of a file when
`G` exists is the same mistake as counting `j` to line 74. Scrolling is what
you do when you cannot name where you are going. The moment you can name it,
name it.

